title:: Stochastica
summary:: Raw maps, flows, stochastic processes, distributions, state processes, and explicit sonifiers
categories:: UGens>Generators>Stochastic, UGens>Generators>Chaotic
related:: Classes/WhiteNoise, Classes/BrownNoise, Classes/LFNoise0, Classes/TIRand, Classes/Stepper

description::
image::scdoc_banner.jpg::

Vanilla SuperCollider provides standard stochastic generators (e.g., WhiteNoise, LFNoise, Dust) and basic chaotic attractors (e.g., HenonC, ChaosGen). However, advanced generative modeling often requires mathematically pure implementations of dynamical systems, non-uniform probability distributions, and state-aware stochastic processes that operate independently of audio-rate interpolation or internal scaling. 

Stochastica introduces a formal mathematical suite for these exact models on the audio server. The collection separates the mathematical simulation of a system from its sonic application. Discrete maps and continuous flows produce raw coordinate trajectories; probability distributions sample from precise statistical models; and stochastic processes maintain memory over time. This structure allows for rigorous algorithmic composition, physical modeling, and complex modulation networks.

A distribution sampler produces independent values when triggered. A stochastic process has memory over time. A discrete map updates a state through a recurrence. A continuous system integrates differential equations. A sonifier maps an internal state to an audible signal and is therefore not the same object as the mathematical system it uses.

Seeded classes use a local PCG32 generator. A nonzero seed is deterministic; zero derives a seed from the parent Synth. Rising triggers and resets use a shared nonpositive-to-positive edge rule.

